 is what makes the diagnosis clinically meaningful and actionable in patient care [Data: Entities (28), Relationships (74, 87)].\",\n            \"summary\": \"Associated symptoms such as nausea, photophobia, and phonophobia are critical for diagnosing migraine without aura\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"The International Headache Classification (IHC) is the authoritative body responsible for publishing the formal diagnostic criteria for migraine without aura. It provides the framework used by clinicians worldwide to diagnose and classify headache disorders. The IHC’s criteria are embedded in key reference tables, such as Table 4, which details the specific diagnostic requirements for migraine without aura, and Table 7, which supports differential diagnosis against other primary headaches [Data: Entities (79, 87), Relationships (81, 87)]. This classification system ensures consistency in diagnosis across different healthcare settings and is foundational to both clinical practice and research [Data: Entities (79), Relationships (81, 87)].\",\n            \"summary\": \"The International Headache Classification provides the authoritative diagnostic framework for migraine without aura\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Migraine without aura is a subtype of primary headache disorders, which are defined as headaches not caused by an underlying disease. This classification is crucial because it distinguishes migraine from secondary headaches that result from other medical conditions. The broader category of primary headache includes migraine, tension-type headache, and cluster headache, each with distinct clinical features and diagnostic criteria [Data: Entities (75, 81, 82), Relationships (74, 93, 94)]. Accurate classification is essential for appropriate treatment planning and patient management, as misdiagnosis can lead to ineffective or harmful interventions [Data: Entities (81, 82), Relationships (93, 94)].\",\n        